  },
  "fallbacks": [
    "Each shipped family is followed by metric-matched fallback faces over local system fonts (size-adjust and ascent, descent, and line-gap overrides), then a system stack and a generic family.",
    "There's a fallback face at each weight the roles set, over the system bold for a heading, and Newsreader's italic falls back to the system italic.",
    "Glyphs outside every shipped face's unicode-range render in the declared system stack, so text never goes missing."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "Newsreader's x-height is 0.426 of its size, under the 0.45 a pack may set, so its roles set font-size-adjust at 0.45: they render 1.06 times the size the layout set, not the 1.17 times the platform's 0.5 would give.",
    "Fraunces ships at its default SOFT 0 and WONK 0, at 600 upright alone, for the headings.",
    "Display, headline and title are 600-only: a composition that sets another weight on them gets the 600 face, so it should move that line to a role whose face exists, such as body or quote. Fraunces 400 would put a Latin page at 106,760 B (17,968 B more), over the 102,400 B font ceiling (LANG-5d).",
    "Running heads and labels use the system monospace: IBM Plex Mono would push the pack past the ceiling.",
    "The latin-ext subsets, 61,944 B together, load only on a page whose text needs them, such as ő or ł; a Latin page loads 88,792 B, Newsreader's 600 for numerals included. Other scripts render in the declared system stack."
  ],
